==Russian==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ru|слонъ}} {{ru-PRO}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ru|sla-pro|*slonъ}}, possibly a deverbative from {{cog|sla-pro|*sloniti sę||to lean against}}, relating to а medieval story of а sleeping elephant who leaned against a tree. According to some other sources, it is related to the {{cog|tr|arslan||lion}}. (Vasmer 1955: 663, Stachowski 2005: 447)

However both etymologies are semantically unlikely, with the former probably originating as a folk etymology. Vovin (2011) proposes that Slavic ''slonъ'' reflects Old Chinese  *''slaŋ'' (象) ‘elephant', an etymology spreviously suggested by Ivanov (1977: 156-57) albeit with an incorrect OC reconstruction *''sðaŋ''. As Vovin notes, contact between Slavic and Old Chinese is out of the question, so the solution might arise from an intermediary source. Ivanov (1977:154) believes that the Chuvash forms ''slon'' ‘elephant’ and ''sălan'' 'id.' are Russian loans, with the latter being called into question by Vovin on phonetic grounds. According to him, Russian /o/ (phonetically a diphthong [uo] with a mid-high syllabic element [o]) is unlikely to be borrowed as Chuvash low vowel /a/. The reverse, namely the borrowing of Bulghar slightly labialised /a/ as Slavic /o/ is more than likely. Chuvash sălan ‘elephant’ is exactly the expected outcome of the OC *slaŋ with the insertion of /ă/ break-ing the OC initial cluster /sl-/ and typical Bulghar shift of PT *ŋ to /n/. The presence of this word in Chuvash places proto-Bulghar speakers in the vicinity of Northern China no later that first century BCE, because approximately after that date the initial clusters in Old Chinese underwent the process of simplification. <ref> Vovin, A. 2011. First and second person singular pronouns: a pillar or a pillory of the ‘Altaic’ hypothesis? </ref>

